 ],
 "literal": "In the old, barren wasteland far beyond the town / where fragrant flowers of bright clusters from the high-standing kino tree / and beautiful leaves of the palmyra palm, they decorated and strung together / as cowherds of many cows adorned you with a foliage wreath / you have become a stone, O lord of swift horses! / The life under the shade of your feet, which resembles a sky-thunderbolt / was the wealth of gift-seekers; besides that / the victory that made kings of elephants with fierce strength / submit, has also departed along with you!",
 "translation": "In the ancient, barren wasteland far beyond the town, where cowherds of many cattle have strung together the bright, fragrant clusters of the tall kino tree and the beautiful leaves of the palmyra palm to adorn you with a foliage wreath, you have now become a hero-stone, O lord of swift horses! The life under the protective shade of your feet, which was like a mighty thunderbolt, was the wealth of those who sought your gifts. Now, not only that wealth, but also the victory that made kings with fierce, strong elephants bow down, has departed along with you!",
 "modern": "ஊருக்கு மிக அப்பால் உள்ள பழமையான, வறண்ட சுடுகாட்டில், உயர்ந்த வேங்கை மரத்தின் பிரகாசமான நறுமணமுள்ள மலர்க்கொத்துகளையும், பனை மரத்தின் அழகிய ஓலைகளையும் தொடுத்து, பல பசுக்களைக் கொண்ட இடையர்கள் உனக்குத் தழையாலான மாலையைச் சூட்ட, நீ ஒரு நடுகல்லாக மாறிவிட்டாயே, வேகமான குதிரைகளைக் கொண்ட தலைவனே! இடி போன்ற உனது திருவடி நிழலில் வாழ்ந்த வாழ்க்கை, உன்னிடம் பரிசு பெற வருவோரின் செல்வமாக இருந்தது. அதுமட்டுமல்லாமல், கொடிய வலிமை கொண்ட யானைகளை உடைய பகை வேந்தர்கள் ஒடுங்கிப் பணியுமாறு நீ பெற்ற வெற்றியும் உன்னுடனேயே சென்றுவிட்டதே!",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the karantai tiṇai (cattle recovery) and the kaiyaṟunilai tuṟai (lamenting a hero's death). The poet, Cōṇāṭṭu Mukaiyalūrc Ciṟukaruntumbiyār, grieves for a fallen chief who has now been memorialized as a hero-stone (naṭukal) in a desolate wasteland far from the town. The cowherds, whose cattle the chief likely rescued, pay tribute by adorning his stone with a simple wreath of wild kino flowers and palmyra leaves. The poet contrasts the chief's past glory—his swift horses, his thunderbolt-like protection, and his victories over powerful kings with fierce elephants—with his current silent state as a cold stone. The key phrases 'kal āyiṉaiyē' ('you have become a stone') and 'veṉṟiyum niṉṉoṭu celavē' ('victory too has departed with you') capture the profound grief and helplessness of the survivors.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
